WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:00.760
Hey friends.

00:00.760 --> 00:05.360
So now we can talk about an advanced topic and that is the execution plan.

00:05.360 --> 00:06.880
It is a very effective tool.

00:06.880 --> 00:11.280
In order to find the exact pain point of why your queries are slow.

00:11.280 --> 00:12.040
So let's go.

00:15.960 --> 00:21.280
All right friends, so now let's say that you have a large, complex analytical SQL query.

00:21.560 --> 00:25.400
And it involves a lot of joins and aggregations and so on.

00:25.600 --> 00:26.920
But it is slow.

00:26.960 --> 00:31.680
And of course you want to go and optimize the performance of your query by maybe using indexes.

00:31.840 --> 00:37.440
And now the big question is where exactly am I going to go build this index on which table on which

00:37.440 --> 00:38.160
columns.

00:38.160 --> 00:41.360
So that means you have to understand where exactly the problem is.

00:41.400 --> 00:45.920
Is it by joining tables or sorting data or by the aggregations?

00:45.960 --> 00:51.400
Now in order to answer all those questions we have something called execution plan.

00:51.680 --> 00:58.840
So what is that the execution plan going to show you how the database exactly process your query step

00:58.840 --> 00:59.560
by step.

00:59.870 --> 01:01.310
and this is what we need.

01:01.350 --> 01:04.910
It's going to show us where exactly we have a performance issue.

01:05.150 --> 01:07.430
So in other words, the execution plan.

01:07.430 --> 01:11.790
It's like your window on how the SQL database thinks.

01:11.790 --> 01:16.710
And once you understand that, then you're going to make a right decision on building an index.

01:16.710 --> 01:18.990
So let's understand exactly what this means.

01:19.030 --> 01:19.310
Okay.

01:19.350 --> 01:24.670
So now let's imagine that you are doing a query like selecting from table and then joining the data

01:24.670 --> 01:25.710
with another table.

01:25.710 --> 01:30.950
So now once you execute this query the database engine will not go immediately and start fetching data

01:30.950 --> 01:31.790
from the disk.

01:31.790 --> 01:35.790
But instead of that first the SQL has to make a plan.

01:35.830 --> 01:41.190
So it's like you are planning a trip where you check the Google map in order to find the best route

01:41.230 --> 01:43.590
in order to reach the destination.

01:43.590 --> 01:46.270
And the execution plan is exactly the same thing.

01:46.430 --> 01:51.950
The database has first to plan how to execute your query, and it's going to build this plan step by

01:51.950 --> 01:55.630
step based on your query and as well the statistics.

01:55.790 --> 01:59.670
So the first step for example how to get the data from the tables.

01:59.670 --> 02:03.790
And there are like multiple ways like scan index or full table scan.

02:03.790 --> 02:09.310
And then after that it need to decide which type of joins can be done, like is it a hash join or a

02:09.310 --> 02:10.070
loop join.

02:10.070 --> 02:13.430
And then at the end of this plan it's going to be the select statements.

02:13.430 --> 02:18.950
So once the execution plan is ready, the database engine can start implementing the steps.

02:18.950 --> 02:22.310
So it's going to go and start reading your tables for example from the disk.

02:22.310 --> 02:24.190
And then after that it's going to join the tables.

02:24.190 --> 02:29.190
And then select the columns and send at the end the results to the end user.

02:29.190 --> 02:34.070
And now once everything is done, the database engine can do one more thing where it's going to go and

02:34.070 --> 02:37.510
take this execution plan and store it at the cache.

02:37.670 --> 02:44.270
And that's because the database engine can go and reuse this plan if we have a similar query.

02:44.270 --> 02:49.350
So for example, if you go and execute the same query again, the database engine here can understand,

02:49.390 --> 02:51.390
ah, this is the same query.

02:51.430 --> 02:54.230
We have already built an execution plan for that.

02:54.230 --> 02:56.190
So it can go and check the cache.

02:56.190 --> 03:00.620
And it is way faster to get it immediately from the cache instead of building it.

03:00.620 --> 03:04.860
So in this scenario, the database engine doesn't have to make any decisions or something like that.

03:04.900 --> 03:10.340
It's going to go and get the plan from the cache and start immediately by executing the plan.

03:10.340 --> 03:14.620
And of course, the database engine will not hide the execution plan from the users.

03:14.780 --> 03:20.140
You can go and check it, because you can go and check how the database loaded the data, how they are

03:20.180 --> 03:21.180
joined, and so on.

03:21.180 --> 03:27.060
And then you can make a correct decision on how to optimize your query, maybe by adding indexes.

03:27.060 --> 03:29.580
So let's go back to SQL and see how we can do that.

03:33.620 --> 03:33.940
Okay.

03:33.940 --> 03:38.380
So now we're going to work with the database Adventureworks 2022.

03:38.420 --> 03:40.140
And now we're going to go to our tables.

03:40.140 --> 03:42.180
And we're going to focus on the facts.

03:42.220 --> 03:43.860
Fact reseller sales.

03:43.900 --> 03:46.220
Now let's go and check the type of this table.

03:46.220 --> 03:52.220
So if you go inside it and go to the indexes you can see that we have an index on the primary key.

03:52.220 --> 03:55.420
So we have a clustered row store index.

03:55.420 --> 03:58.340
So this means that data is structured in this B-tree.

03:58.380 --> 04:02.340
So now what we're going to do, we're going to go and create a mirror of this table, but without any

04:02.340 --> 04:03.020
indexes.

04:03.020 --> 04:04.420
So it's going to be very simple.

04:04.420 --> 04:09.380
Select star from our fact reseller sales.

04:09.540 --> 04:11.740
And we're going to insert it in a new table.

04:11.740 --> 04:16.980
So into fact reseller sales.

04:17.020 --> 04:19.300
And I'm going to call it HP for heap.

04:19.300 --> 04:20.900
So let's go and execute it.

04:20.900 --> 04:24.740
And now you can see we have inserted in the new table around 60,000 rows.

04:24.780 --> 04:28.540
So now we can go and refresh our tables in order to find our new table.

04:28.740 --> 04:31.180
So it is over here factory seller sales.

04:31.180 --> 04:34.340
And if you check the indexes you will not find any.

04:34.340 --> 04:36.300
So that means it is a heap table.

04:36.340 --> 04:40.620
Now let's go and do a very simple query on top of our new table.

04:40.620 --> 04:45.460
So select star from the factory seller HP like this.

04:45.460 --> 04:46.900
So let's go and execute it.

04:46.900 --> 04:48.420
And we got the results.

04:48.420 --> 04:52.540
So now the question is I would like to see the execution plan of this query.

04:52.580 --> 04:58.370
Now in order to see the execution plan we're going to go to the toolbar over here and we have three

04:58.370 --> 04:58.890
things.

04:59.050 --> 05:03.170
The first one is says display estimated execution plan.

05:03.170 --> 05:07.690
And we have another one says include actual execution plan.

05:07.690 --> 05:11.490
And a third one says include live query statistics.

05:11.530 --> 05:14.210
So now the question is what are the differences between them?

05:14.250 --> 05:18.010
Let's start with the first one displayed estimated execution plan.

05:18.050 --> 05:23.250
So here what's going to happen is SQL is going to go and guess the execution plan without executing

05:23.250 --> 05:24.050
the query.

05:24.050 --> 05:25.770
So it's just an estimation.

05:25.770 --> 05:28.370
So this is only a guess an estimation.

05:28.410 --> 05:30.690
The second one is the actual one.

05:30.690 --> 05:36.690
So this is going to show you the execution plan that is used in order to process your query.

05:36.690 --> 05:41.730
So after executing your query SQL going to show for you which plan is used.

05:41.730 --> 05:43.530
So that means the estimated plan.

05:43.530 --> 05:46.770
It is something before executing your query.

05:47.090 --> 05:51.090
And the actual plan is something after executing your query.

05:51.090 --> 05:54.370
And the third one is while executing the query.

05:54.370 --> 06:00.810
So you're gonna get real time execution of your query, and you can see how your execution plan is working.

06:00.810 --> 06:02.250
So now we can go and try that.

06:02.250 --> 06:05.250
Let's go and activate the estimated execution plan.

06:05.290 --> 06:09.810
Now we can see over here we have a new output where you can see like few boxes.

06:09.810 --> 06:14.250
So this is an estimated execution plan without executing your query.

06:14.250 --> 06:19.530
But now if you go over here and switch it to the actual execution plan, nothing gonna happen.

06:19.530 --> 06:21.530
Because first you have to execute the query.

06:21.530 --> 06:23.090
So let's go and do that.

06:23.090 --> 06:26.770
So once we have executed we got the result the messages.

06:26.770 --> 06:29.730
And here we have a new tab called execution plan.

06:29.730 --> 06:35.570
So if you go over here you will find the real execution plan that is used to process your query.

06:35.570 --> 06:39.530
And let's go and try the third one and let's go and execute.

06:40.450 --> 06:42.970
It was pretty fast because the query is very fast.

06:42.970 --> 06:48.530
But here we can see how the data and the plan is working during the execution.

06:48.530 --> 06:50.930
So this is the live execution plan.

06:50.930 --> 06:55.320
And of course we have the last one which is the current execution plan.

06:55.360 --> 06:58.080
So those are the differences between those stuff.

06:58.120 --> 07:03.480
Now you might ask why do we have this estimated and actual execution plans?

07:03.520 --> 07:08.480
Well, it is a really nice tool to understand whether everything like is healthy at your database,

07:08.480 --> 07:14.880
because if the guessing is something else as the actual execution plan, that means this is an indicator

07:14.880 --> 07:19.400
that something is wrong at the statistics or the index at your database.

07:19.480 --> 07:23.960
So if they are matching the estimated and the actual, then everything looks good.

07:24.000 --> 07:27.560
But now we're going to focus only on one type of those execution plans.

07:27.560 --> 07:30.880
We're going to stick with the actual execution plan.

07:30.920 --> 07:31.760
So now what we're going to do.

07:31.760 --> 07:37.320
We're going to go and open two queries side by side, and one going to be from the clustered index.

07:37.320 --> 07:39.920
And another one is from the heap structure.

07:39.920 --> 07:41.640
So it's going to be like 1 to 1.

07:41.680 --> 07:43.760
Let's go and query both of them.

07:43.760 --> 07:46.840
And now let's go and try to read the execution plan.

07:46.840 --> 07:50.560
But make sure that you are activating the actual execution plan.

07:50.600 --> 07:53.800
So we have here now two plants.

07:53.800 --> 07:57.200
So now we are at the table and we don't have any indexes.

07:57.200 --> 08:00.320
So now the question is how to read this execution plan.

08:00.320 --> 08:03.840
Well now the plan is very simple because we have a very simple query.

08:03.840 --> 08:06.400
But we read it from the right to the left.

08:06.400 --> 08:09.680
So the first operation is the table scan.

08:09.680 --> 08:13.840
And then we have here a very small arrow to the next one where we have the select.

08:13.840 --> 08:15.200
So from right to left.

08:15.200 --> 08:19.280
So now of course the first operator is how to read your data inside the table.

08:19.280 --> 08:21.200
And here we have different types of scans.

08:21.200 --> 08:23.200
And one of them is the table scan.

08:23.200 --> 08:27.280
So table scan actually is scanning the entire table.

08:27.280 --> 08:32.560
So it's going to go and scan all the rows inside your tables in order to execute this query.

08:32.600 --> 08:39.640
Now if you go and mouse hover on the table scan, you will find a lot of details about what is happening

08:39.640 --> 08:42.520
during loading the data or scanning the table.

08:42.560 --> 08:44.040
But it is a little bit annoying.

08:44.040 --> 08:49.080
Better than that, if you go right click on it and then go to properties, you will get in the right

08:49.080 --> 08:52.230
side the same details, but it is easier to read.

08:52.230 --> 08:56.550
So the first thing that we have to read is the number of rows that has been read.

08:56.550 --> 09:01.070
So we can see that we have read all the rows inside the table, which is not really good.

09:01.110 --> 09:04.350
And we have another important information about the resources and the cost.

09:04.350 --> 09:07.590
So we have the CPU cost and the input output costs.

09:07.590 --> 09:13.710
And what is interesting is the logical operator that tables can and we can see some nice informations

09:13.710 --> 09:14.550
about the storage.

09:14.550 --> 09:16.230
It says it is roster.

09:16.350 --> 09:22.110
Now let's go and check the execution plan of this other table where we have a clustered index.

09:22.110 --> 09:24.350
So let's go to the execution plan.

09:24.430 --> 09:27.190
And now you can see that we have on the right side something else.

09:27.190 --> 09:28.470
We don't have table scan.

09:28.470 --> 09:31.750
We have something called clustered index scan.

09:31.750 --> 09:38.630
It is either scanning the entire table again or only a range or a part of the index.

09:38.670 --> 09:43.070
And of course in the details we can see whether it read all the informations or not.

09:43.110 --> 09:48.990
Now if you go and check the number of rows again, the whole index is read in order to get this results.

09:48.990 --> 09:51.990
So again we have here the total number of rows inside our table.

09:52.190 --> 09:56.510
And as you can see over here the logical operation is clustered index scan.

09:56.550 --> 09:58.150
So it is not table scan.

09:58.190 --> 10:03.270
Now of course we have to go and check the CPU and the input output costs whether we are consuming the

10:03.270 --> 10:04.630
same efforts or not.

10:04.670 --> 10:06.270
So we can go and compare stuff.

10:06.270 --> 10:09.270
So here we have like 0.07.

10:09.310 --> 10:14.910
And if we go over here you can see we didn't gain like a lot of information having an index on this

10:14.910 --> 10:15.430
table.

10:15.430 --> 10:19.670
And that's of course logical because this query is not using any indexes.

10:19.670 --> 10:22.630
It is just like selecting everything from the whole table.

10:26.630 --> 10:30.470
So now let's go and extend it where we're going to sort the data by the primary key.

10:30.630 --> 10:33.590
Sales order number.

10:34.190 --> 10:35.870
So let's go and get this one.

10:35.870 --> 10:38.550
And as well for the heap structure.

10:38.550 --> 10:42.070
So let's go and execute it and check the execution plan.

10:42.070 --> 10:46.070
And the same thing for our cluster table.

10:46.110 --> 10:48.390
Now let's check first the heap structure.

10:48.390 --> 10:50.620
As you can see here, we have like two steps.

10:50.860 --> 10:54.060
First it's going to go and scan the whole table.

10:54.060 --> 11:00.300
And then we have a sort operator in order to go and sort all the data in order to present it in the

11:00.300 --> 11:00.900
output.

11:00.940 --> 11:04.220
And at the end we have the select which is not really important.

11:04.220 --> 11:06.060
So here we have like two operators.

11:06.060 --> 11:10.940
But now if you go to our clustered index you can see that we have only like two steps.

11:10.940 --> 11:13.380
There is no sort step right.

11:13.380 --> 11:20.380
And that's because the clustered index is only sorted and SQL don't have to go and sort the data again.

11:20.380 --> 11:22.740
So it doesn't have to go and sort anything.

11:22.740 --> 11:24.140
The data is already sorted.

11:24.140 --> 11:28.380
So this is the first win that you have if you have an index.

11:28.380 --> 11:30.180
So everything is already sorted.

11:30.180 --> 11:35.220
And if you have an order by on this column then SQL don't have to do it during the query.

11:35.220 --> 11:39.340
So now if you want to go and compare the costs, you can see here we still have the same cost for the

11:39.340 --> 11:45.980
CPU and the input output in the heap structure without any index we have here, like double cost.

11:46.020 --> 11:48.620
The first cost is for the table scan.

11:48.620 --> 11:54.220
It is the exact same amount of CPU and input output like the clustered, but as well.

11:54.260 --> 12:00.860
On top of it we have high cost for sorting the data, so we are consuming more CPU and input output.

12:00.860 --> 12:07.500
And if you summarize those costs, of course this query is going to be slower and bad compared to the

12:07.500 --> 12:08.780
clustered index.

12:08.780 --> 12:13.780
So with that in the execution plan you can understand exactly the benefit of your index.

12:13.780 --> 12:16.660
And one more thing about this plan if you go over here.

12:17.020 --> 12:23.740
So if you go to the objects and let me just extend it like this, you can see the name of the index

12:23.740 --> 12:25.900
that has been used for your query.

12:25.900 --> 12:29.980
So it says the index is PKI for primary key.

12:30.020 --> 12:31.420
And then we have the whole thing.

12:31.420 --> 12:35.180
So now if you go to our table on the left side check the indexes.

12:35.300 --> 12:37.620
It's going to be exactly this index.

12:37.740 --> 12:43.420
So in the execution plan you can find as well which index has been used in your query.

12:43.420 --> 12:45.220
And this is very important to check.

12:45.340 --> 12:47.130
If you create a new index.

12:47.130 --> 12:52.170
Then run your query and check whether the database is using your new created index.

12:52.170 --> 12:55.770
And if not, then you are making the wrong decisions about your index.

12:55.770 --> 13:00.690
So each time you create a new index, make sure to check whether in the execution plan the database

13:00.730 --> 13:02.650
is using your new index.

13:06.530 --> 13:07.930
Okay, so now let's keep going.

13:07.970 --> 13:13.210
Now instead of using the primary key I'm going to go and filter the data based on one of those columns

13:13.210 --> 13:14.330
that we have in this table.

13:14.330 --> 13:15.890
So let me check the results.

13:15.930 --> 13:20.010
And let's take for example the carrier tracking number.

13:20.010 --> 13:26.530
So carrier tracking number and let's go and pick a value the first one here like this.

13:26.570 --> 13:31.410
And let's do the same thing for the heap table and execute it.

13:31.450 --> 13:35.210
And now in the execution plan you see we still have a table scan.

13:35.210 --> 13:39.890
And on this table let's see the execution plan with the clustered index.

13:39.890 --> 13:44.730
Now let's say that I would like to go and create a non-clustered index for this column.

13:44.730 --> 13:45.690
So let's go and do it.

13:45.800 --> 13:49.640
So create non-clustered index.

13:49.640 --> 13:53.040
And I'm going to call it index fact reseller.

13:53.400 --> 13:55.360
And then the column name.

13:55.600 --> 14:02.400
So on our table Facts reseller and the column going to be carrier tracking number.

14:02.400 --> 14:06.840
So I'm going to take it from here and let's go and create it.

14:06.840 --> 14:10.040
Now let's see whether our query can use this index.

14:10.040 --> 14:11.720
So let's go and execute it.

14:11.720 --> 14:13.520
And let's go to the execution plan.

14:13.560 --> 14:16.320
Now things looks completely different than before.

14:16.440 --> 14:17.960
So what is going on.

14:17.960 --> 14:19.960
We can see that we have now something new.

14:20.000 --> 14:22.000
We don't have a clustered index.

14:22.000 --> 14:24.480
We have something called index seq.

14:24.560 --> 14:31.920
Index seq is an amazing sign in your execution plan, because it tells us the SQL server did find a

14:31.920 --> 14:38.000
way to use the index in order to find the exact data that we need, without scanning a lot of stuff.

14:38.000 --> 14:40.720
So that means now we have like three types of scans.

14:40.720 --> 14:45.240
We have the table scan where the SQL is going to go and scan the whole table.

14:45.240 --> 14:47.440
And this can happen in the heap structure.

14:47.440 --> 14:50.240
And the second one we have the index scan.

14:50.240 --> 14:55.480
And here we don't know whether it is scanning the whole index or a part of the index.

14:55.480 --> 15:01.920
And the last one we have the index seq where the database is able to find directly the data without

15:01.920 --> 15:03.240
scanning a lot of stuff.

15:03.240 --> 15:05.640
So the worst type is the table scan.

15:05.840 --> 15:07.760
Then we have the index scan.

15:07.760 --> 15:10.600
And the best one is the index seq.

15:10.880 --> 15:16.840
So if you check here the details you can see the number of rows that has been read is only 12.

15:16.880 --> 15:18.000
This is amazing.

15:18.040 --> 15:20.280
Let's go and check the heap scan over here.

15:20.280 --> 15:22.480
So to the execution plan.

15:22.480 --> 15:29.880
And if we go over here you can see that we are reading around 60,000 rows in order to get 12.

15:29.920 --> 15:34.560
But with the index we are reading only 12 in order to get 12.

15:34.600 --> 15:37.320
And this is amazing and very fast of course.

15:37.360 --> 15:40.160
And of course the cost of this is very, very small.

15:40.160 --> 15:45.470
So if you check the CPU and the input output you can see those numbers are nothing.

15:45.630 --> 15:49.510
And of course, if you go to the objects over here, you can see which index has been used.

15:49.510 --> 15:53.070
And this is exactly the index that we have just created.

15:53.070 --> 15:57.310
So that means it was a really good decision to create this index.

15:57.310 --> 16:02.590
And the SQL was very happy about it and used it in order to fast find our data.

16:02.590 --> 16:04.430
So now let's go and check the rest of the plan.

16:04.430 --> 16:06.990
And now you can see over here we have key lookup.

16:06.990 --> 16:12.070
The key lookup is an operation that we need in order to get the rest of the columns.

16:12.070 --> 16:17.990
Because from this index we are getting the data of only one column, the carrier tracking number.

16:17.990 --> 16:22.830
But since in our query we are saying select star, that means we have a lot of columns.

16:22.830 --> 16:25.390
And those columns are not part of the index.

16:25.390 --> 16:29.110
So in this index SQL don't know anything about the rest.

16:29.110 --> 16:32.870
That's why SQL has to go and search for the other columns.

16:32.870 --> 16:37.070
And of course it is called a lookup not a scan or something like that.

16:37.110 --> 16:40.670
And that's why we have here as well only 12 rows.

16:40.710 --> 16:44.030
But from this step we will get the rest of the columns.

16:44.350 --> 16:49.310
So and now the next step of that is going to go and join those two informations.

16:49.310 --> 16:55.030
So we have from the first one the carrier tracking number and the second one we have the rest of course

16:55.110 --> 16:59.350
SQL has to go and merge all those stuff in one in order to have it as a results.

16:59.390 --> 17:02.710
And now this operation called a nested loops.

17:02.710 --> 17:07.670
Behind the scenes there are different types of joins, not the one that we know, the inner lift and

17:07.670 --> 17:08.270
so on.

17:08.270 --> 17:10.310
But there is another types of joins.

17:10.310 --> 17:11.950
We have the nested loop.

17:11.950 --> 17:14.710
We have the merge join and the hash join.

17:14.710 --> 17:17.790
The nested loop is very good for small stuff.

17:17.790 --> 17:23.710
If you have large tables, then the merge and the hash joins are way better than the nested loop.

17:23.710 --> 17:30.310
So that means if you are getting here a lot of data from the index and the lookups, and you see SQL

17:30.310 --> 17:32.910
is using a nested loop, this is not good.

17:32.950 --> 17:38.070
But for now it is okay because we are getting only 12 rows and the operation is going to be fast enough.

17:38.350 --> 17:43.140
And now one more thing that we can see inside our execution plan is the cost in percentage.

17:43.260 --> 17:47.500
So from checking this plan, you can see the select is almost costing nothing.

17:47.820 --> 17:51.380
The cost of the nested loop is as well like 0%.

17:51.420 --> 17:54.700
And then we have like 6% of the indexiq.

17:54.780 --> 18:01.780
Thus, because it is pretty fast and the most expensive operation that done in our query is the key

18:01.780 --> 18:05.860
lookups of course, because it can go and get all the columns.

18:05.900 --> 18:10.620
And now if you go and compare it to the heap structure, even though that the execution plan of the

18:10.620 --> 18:16.740
heap structure looks very small, doesn't mean that it's faster than the indexes that we have.

18:16.780 --> 18:23.140
Still, if you go and add up all those numbers, it is way, way faster than the heap structure.

18:23.180 --> 18:24.780
Now I would like to show you one more thing.

18:24.780 --> 18:31.540
If you want to get rid of this key lookup, and in your query you have only selecting the carrier tracking

18:31.540 --> 18:31.900
number.

18:31.940 --> 18:34.780
Let's go and execute it and go to the execution plan.

18:34.780 --> 18:40.780
As you can see, there is no need for the lookup because we have only one column and this data we can

18:40.780 --> 18:43.060
get it completely from our index.

18:43.060 --> 18:48.380
So as you can see, it is interesting to understand how SQL is working with your table and with your

18:48.380 --> 18:49.020
index.

18:49.020 --> 18:54.300
And this is how to validate whether you are making correct decisions about your indexes.

18:58.300 --> 18:58.540
Okay.

18:58.540 --> 19:03.100
So now let's go and add more stuff where we are doing aggregation joins and so on.

19:03.300 --> 19:04.660
Let's extend our query.

19:04.660 --> 19:07.220
So I'm going to go and join it with another dimension.

19:07.220 --> 19:13.660
Like for example the dim products and the join going to be on the product key.

19:13.820 --> 19:18.340
So products key and equal to as well product key.

19:18.660 --> 19:21.340
Now after that we're going to go and aggregate view stuff.

19:21.340 --> 19:23.700
So we're going to aggregate by the product name.

19:24.020 --> 19:25.820
So I'm going to take the product name.

19:25.820 --> 19:28.580
So it's going to be the English product name.

19:28.620 --> 19:32.260
And let's go and call it product name.

19:32.260 --> 19:34.220
And let's go and aggregate the sales.

19:34.220 --> 19:37.900
So sum and we're going to get it from the fact table.

19:37.940 --> 19:39.970
It's going to be sales amount.

19:40.010 --> 19:49.050
So as total sales and of course we have to go and do group by and not the French name, it's going to

19:49.050 --> 19:50.290
be the English name.

19:51.530 --> 19:55.690
So let's go up by the product name and that's it.

19:55.690 --> 19:57.010
Let's go and execute it.

19:57.050 --> 19:59.850
Now we have a nice list of products and total sales.

19:59.850 --> 20:02.330
But let's go and check the execution plan.

20:02.330 --> 20:05.130
And oh my god we have a lot of stuff.

20:05.130 --> 20:07.170
So let's start from the right side.

20:07.170 --> 20:09.650
So let's do it quickly from the right to the left.

20:09.650 --> 20:13.490
So the first thing is that it's going to go and get the data from the facts.

20:13.490 --> 20:15.890
So it is using the clustered index.

20:15.890 --> 20:20.650
And then after that it's going to go and do a hash match for the aggregation.

20:20.650 --> 20:25.970
And after that it's going to go and sort the data because it is doing later a merge join.

20:25.970 --> 20:29.090
So all those steps are preparing the fact table.

20:29.090 --> 20:32.650
And then we have another clustered scan for the dimension.

20:32.650 --> 20:33.450
So it's going to go.

20:33.450 --> 20:36.090
And as well select the informations from the dimension.

20:36.090 --> 20:39.610
And we have here like not a lot of rows, so it is very small table.

20:39.650 --> 20:40.730
600 rows.

20:40.770 --> 20:45.050
And now of course the result of the cluster scan is as well sorted.

20:45.050 --> 20:45.490
Right.

20:45.490 --> 20:48.890
And of course as we learned the clustered index can go and sort the data.

20:48.890 --> 20:53.610
So we have here a sorted output together with another sorted output.

20:53.610 --> 20:55.770
So we have like two data sets that are sorted.

20:55.770 --> 21:02.530
And SQL here decided to go with the merge join which is a good join in order to join two sorted datasets.

21:02.730 --> 21:06.290
It is way faster than joining using the nested loop.

21:06.290 --> 21:10.610
So everything is fine and then the data can be sorted and presented as output.

21:10.650 --> 21:16.290
And now if you are checking this plan, you can see the most expensive thing happened at the fact table.

21:16.290 --> 21:20.930
So 71% of the total cost happened in this step.

21:20.970 --> 21:24.690
Now let's say that the query is slow and I would like to go and optimize it.

21:24.730 --> 21:32.010
We have learned that if you are doing aggregations on big tables, then the Columnstore index is a good

21:32.010 --> 21:32.650
idea.

21:32.650 --> 21:35.290
So let's go and find whether that is true.

21:35.290 --> 21:38.320
So I'm going to go to our other table.

21:38.480 --> 21:41.440
So our sales table was with the heap structure.

21:41.440 --> 21:42.840
And now you say, you know what.

21:42.880 --> 21:46.400
Let's go and convert this heap structure to a column store.

21:46.440 --> 21:47.560
So let's go and do that.

21:47.560 --> 21:53.120
So we're going to say create clustered column store index.

21:53.360 --> 21:55.120
And we're going to call it index.

21:55.120 --> 22:00.200
And then the whole name fact reseller sales HP.

22:00.320 --> 22:02.280
And we don't have to specify any columns.

22:02.280 --> 22:05.720
So it's going to be our table on.

22:05.760 --> 22:06.600
And that's it.

22:06.640 --> 22:08.520
Let's go and execute it.

22:08.560 --> 22:11.720
So now our table is not anymore heap structure.

22:11.880 --> 22:13.800
It should be a column store.

22:13.840 --> 22:19.760
So if you go and check the informations we can see we have like clustered column stored index on it.

22:19.760 --> 22:23.760
So now let's go and do the same query and check whether we have a better performance.

22:23.800 --> 22:25.240
Let's go and execute it.

22:25.240 --> 22:28.200
And of course you have to go and activate the execution plan.

22:28.200 --> 22:28.960
So I'm going to.

22:29.480 --> 22:32.760
And now let's go and check from the right again.

22:32.760 --> 22:34.960
So this is our fact table.

22:34.960 --> 22:38.880
And as you can see already it is costing only 6%.

22:38.920 --> 22:39.600
Interesting.

22:39.600 --> 22:41.960
So let's go and compare what happened to our fact table.

22:42.000 --> 22:46.680
First of all, we can see that the physical operation is a columnstore index scan.

22:46.720 --> 22:52.120
And if you go to the objects over here, you can see that the SQL did use the Columnstore.

22:52.160 --> 22:56.680
And that is of course going to happen because the whole data is stored only in the index.

22:56.680 --> 22:58.240
So there is no way around it.

22:58.240 --> 23:00.400
So it can go of course, and use the index.

23:00.400 --> 23:04.680
But now what is interesting, maybe we have to go and compare the CPU costs.

23:04.680 --> 23:09.200
So if we check over here it is like zero comma 0067.

23:09.240 --> 23:11.400
Almost the same thing for the input output.

23:11.440 --> 23:16.080
Let's go to the previous plan where we don't have a columnstore and check our facts.

23:16.080 --> 23:22.440
So as you can see here, it is way more expensive reading the fact table than the columnstore.

23:22.560 --> 23:25.600
And as well we have reduced the input output costs.

23:25.600 --> 23:34.280
So as you can see, we went from 71% of total costs for the fact table to only 6% and the resources

23:34.280 --> 23:36.550
that is used to execute the query.

23:36.590 --> 23:40.510
It is way less than a normal clustered rowstore.

23:40.550 --> 23:43.310
And this is exactly the power of this index.

23:43.310 --> 23:44.670
That columnstore index.

23:44.710 --> 23:49.950
You can use it in big tables like the fact tables like we are doing here in this query.

23:49.990 --> 23:53.710
You will be getting amazing performance for this scenario.

23:53.710 --> 23:58.070
So of course you can go and compare the execution plan by moving left and right.

23:58.070 --> 24:03.630
So as you can see, if I click over here and I just switch to the other tab, I can like quickly compare

24:03.630 --> 24:04.350
the numbers.

24:04.350 --> 24:07.230
But there is another way on how to compare execution plans.

24:07.270 --> 24:13.790
And that is if you go to the execution plan and right click on it, then go to Save Execution Plan as.

24:13.910 --> 24:18.350
And then you have to go and give it a name, for example query Rowstore.

24:18.550 --> 24:20.590
So let's go and save it.

24:20.590 --> 24:24.070
And then you can go to the second query where we have the row store.

24:24.070 --> 24:29.070
And then right click on the execution plan and say compare Showplan.

24:29.110 --> 24:35.390
So once you click on that then you have to go and select the one that you want to compare with so open.

24:35.390 --> 24:37.710
And now on top you have your query.

24:37.710 --> 24:41.550
And at the bottom you have the execution plan that you have saved.

24:41.550 --> 24:47.710
And then you have here a lot of informations where they compare both of the execution plan.

24:47.830 --> 24:51.750
And with that you can go in more details in order to understand which plan is better.

24:51.790 --> 24:56.150
All right friends, so as you can see having the execution plan is is amazing.

24:56.150 --> 24:59.230
We can see how the SQL is working behind the scenes.

24:59.230 --> 25:06.070
And we can understand how SQL is processing my queries step by step, how much resources it is consuming,

25:06.190 --> 25:10.910
whether my indexes are useful or useless, and I can go and experiment stuff.

25:10.910 --> 25:16.310
I can go and add like an index, then test and check whether I gained like few performance or not.

25:16.310 --> 25:22.790
And we can go and compare like multiple execution plans before and after until you get the right index

25:22.790 --> 25:24.990
for the right table and the right column.

25:24.990 --> 25:32.230
So the execution plan are amazing in order to help us understanding whether our indexing strategy is

25:32.230 --> 25:33.310
correct or not.
